Not generic and not overstated — the defect is an asymmetry inside one specific function, not a missing-error-handling platitude.

Confirmed from source:
1. app/(tabs)/index.tsx:71 destructures only `{ data }` from `supabase.rpc('nearby_moves', ...)`, while the sibling path in src/hooks/useSpots.ts:19-27 destructures `{ data, error }` and does `if (error) throw error`. The client at src/lib/supabase.ts:14-21 is created without `throwOnError`, so supabase-js resolves network failures into `error` rather than rejecting; the moves path therefore sees `data === null` and line 78 runs `setMoves([])`, wiping the markers rendered at :158-167.
2. `isEmpty` at :139 is `!isLoading && moves.length === 0`, and :83 `setIsLoading(false)` runs unconditionally on that branch, so the card at :268-282 renders the app-authored copy "No moves in view — Pan around, widen the vibe, or set the first one." That copy is a specific factual assertion about the area, and the advice it gives (pan) re-enters onRegionChangeComplete at :102-106, issuing more failing requests. This is codebase-specific: the failure and the genuine-empty result are made indistinguishable by the shared `moves.length === 0` gate.
3. Spots branch confirmed: `fetchLayer` (:66-84) has no try/catch and no `finally`; `setIsLoading(false)` sits at :83 after the awaited call, so a throw from fetchNearbySpots (useSpots.ts:26) skips it. `isLoading` stays true, `isEmpty` at :139 is gated on `!isLoading` so no empty card renders, and the ActivityIndicator at :284-288 stays up. The rejected promise is caught by nothing: `fetchLayer(region)` is called bare at :90, :99 and :105, and the only ErrorBoundary (app/_layout.tsx:84, src/components/ui/ErrorBoundary.tsx:12) is a React error boundary, which does not catch async rejections. No global unhandledrejection handler exists in the repo.

Two precision notes that do not change the verdict: the stuck spinner also clears if the user taps back to "🕺 Moves" (:213-217), since that effect re-runs fetchLayer down the non-throwing branch — "indefinitely" holds only while the user stays on the Spots layer. And the same effect at :87-91 fires on every category-pill tap (:242-250), so the stuck-spinner state is reachable by filter taps too, not only the layer tap.

Fix (both defects in one edit at :66-84): destructure `error` on the moves RPC and route both branches through shared error state —
```
const [loadError, setLoadError] = useState<string | null>(null);
const fetchLayer = useCallback(async (region: Region) => {
  const radiusM = Math.min(Math.max(regionToMeters(region), 5000), 50_000);
  try {
    if (layer === 'moves') {
      const { data, error } = await supabase.rpc('nearby_moves', {...});
      if (error) throw error;
      setMoves((data ?? []) as NearbyMove[]);
    } else {
      setSpots(await fetchNearbySpots(region.latitude, region.longitude, radiusM, spotCategory));
    }
    setLoadError(null);
  } catch (e) {
    setLoadError('Couldn’t load — check your connection');
  } finally {
    setIsLoading(false);
  }
}, [layer, category, spotCategory]);
```
then gate the card at :268 on `isEmpty && !loadError` and render a retry card when `loadError` is set, so a failed fetch never claims "No moves in view" and never leaves a bare spinner. Leaving `setMoves([])` out of the failure path also keeps the previously loaded pins on screen instead of clearing them.
